  "num_turns": null,
  "final_message": "I fixed the bug in `session_ttl_expired`, and `python3 -m unittest -v test_public.py` now passes (1 of 1 test).\n\n**The bug:** the function stripped the timezone from `now` but not from `created`. With `+00:00` timestamps, that meant subtracting a datetime with no timezone from one that has one, which Python rejects with a `TypeError`.\n\n**The fix:** I removed the `.replace(tzinfo=None)` call on line 10, so both timestamps are parsed the same way.\n\nExtra checks I ran:\n- 30 seconds elapsed with a 60-second TTL returns `False`.\n- `00:00+00:00` to `01:01+01:00` returns `True`, so different offsets compare correctly.\n- Two timestamps with no timezone at all still work.\n\nI left `format_expiry_message` and `test_public.py` untouched. The formatter still flags one long line in the `ttl_seconds` check; that was there before my change, so I didn't reformat it.\n\nDONE: Fixed `session_ttl_expired` so it no longer strips the timezone from `now` only, which crashed on `+00:00` timestamps; the public test now passes.",
