      "final_message": "I rewrote `merge_intervals` in `solution.py` to follow the README, and `python3 -m unittest -v test_public.py` passes (2 of 2 tests).\n\nWhat the function now does:\n- **Wrong input type:** raises `TypeError` if `intervals` isn't a list.\n- **Bad intervals:** raises `ValueError` for any item that isn't a two-element list or tuple, has a non-int or bool value, or has start greater than end.\n- **Merging:** sorts intervals by start, then merges any that overlap or touch (for example, `[1,2]` and `[2,3]` become `[1,3]`). It returns a new list of `[start, end]` lists, and empty input gives `[]`.\n- **No side effects:** the input list and its intervals are never changed.\n\nThe public tests only cover one merge and one invalid interval. I also ran quick one-off checks for the other rules and all behaved as documented: empty input, touching intervals, unsorted input, each kind of bad interval, a tuple passed instead of a list, and confirming the input is left unchanged.\n\nDONE: Rewrote merge_intervals to validate input per the README, sort and merge overlapping or touching intervals into a new list without mutating the input; public tests pass.",
